  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 211 PM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThe long term starts to bring in another unsettled, active weather \nperiod. The first weather feature of note will be the remnants of \nwhat is currently Tropical Storm Vance. The upper level flow begins \nto drop and turn more southerly on Thursday, allowing for more \ndirect advection of the tropical moisture. A weak vorticity lobe \ndevelops within the flow and continues to slowly drag up the \nremnants into portions of southeast and east central NM Thursday \nnight into Friday. As the overall flow is rather weak (20-30kts at \n500mb), the moisture looks to take its time moving through the parts \nof eastern NM. Forecast soundings within the moisture show a nearly \nfully moist-adiabat throughout the air column, signaling low to \nno instability and widespread moderate rain. Current 24 hour NBM \npercentiles (ending Friday afternoon) for rainfall have most \nlikely rainfall amounts at roughly 1-1.5\" from a line of Roswell \nto Tucumcari eastward, with high end amounts entering the 2-3\" \nrange. While the rainfall rates are not expected to be significant\ngiven the lack of instability, soils are likely to be saturated \nfrom previous rainfall events, leading to a quicker onset of \nflooding in some areas.\n\nA second pocket of vorticity lags behind the initial slug of \ntropical moisture, sparking additional showers and storms across \nportions of central and western NM Friday afternoon into Saturday. \nThis second edition of storms does not look to have the energy that \nthe tropical moisture has, and NBM QPF is much lower as a result. \nMost likely rainfall amounts from this second punch sit between 0.25-\n0.5\", primarily around the Gila Region in Catron County.\n\nAfter the two pieces of vorticity move out of the region through the \nweekend, attention turns to two areas of low pressure that are \nforecast to approach New Mexico late Saturday into Sunday. An \ninitial low pressure spinning over the Intermountain West looks to \ninteract with a fast-approaching low coming off of the Pacific \nOcean. These two features are slated to bring additional rainfall to \nparts of western NM and the Four Corners region Saturday night \nthrough at least Sunday evening. Given the placement of the more \nnorthern low, and how the southern low pressure looks to move into \nthe region, a component of upslope flow likely favors higher \nrainfall amounts near terrain. There remains high uncertainty in \nlocation of the heaviest rainfall, as well as the amounts \nthemselves. The primary driver in this is the path of each low and \nhow they may phase with each other to become one. Ensemble cluster \nanalysis is in good agreement on a troughing pattern developing \nthrough the weekend, though the strength and placement of it will \ndictate the extent of rainfall. A faster, more progressive trough \nwould bring overall less rainfall amounts to the area while a slower \none would allow for more rainfall. As it stands now, the most likely \nrainfall falls between 0.5-1\" across western NM locations, with \nhigher amounts of 1-1.5\" closer to higher terrain.\n\nHow this low progresses across NM also dictates the weather to begin \nthe first week of November. Models are split on whether the low \nbecomes closed or remains an open wave, and this would have an \neffect on any potential wrap around precipitation on the northern\nand western side of the low. 700mb temperatures look to drop to \njust above freezing, so this could spell a few inches of snow \nacross the higher peaks of the northern mountains through the \nweekend and into next week.",
